Yes. The deeper I look, the more it seems **prehistoric wildlife is only one unusually good instance of a much larger opportunity**: domains where the internet already contains a half-built machine-readable world, but nobody has turned it into a simulation/content universe.

I would prioritize domains using five criteria:

**Data depth × interaction richness × visual richness × combinatorial content × defensibility.**

## The strongest goldmines

| Rank  | World                                             | Why it fits                                                                          |   My score |
| ----- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------: |
| **1** | Living/extinct animals + ecosystems               | absurd amount of structured data; interactions are inherently simulatable            |  **10/10** |
| **2** | Ancient worlds + mythology                        | places, artifacts, people, iconography, texts and myths already exist as linked data | **9.5/10** |
| **3** | Deep sea                                          | bizarre entities, real environments, huge discovery factor, excellent APIs           | **9.3/10** |
| **4** | Space / planetary systems                         | probably the most computationally exact simulation domain                            | **9.2/10** |
| **5** | Evolution / Tree of Life                          | millions of entities and real relationships; enormous educational/game space         | **9.0/10** |
| **6** | Natural Earth events                              | volcanoes, earthquakes, climate/environment interactions                             | **8.5/10** |
| **7** | Archaeological objects / ancient material culture | enormous public-domain visual libraries + linked history                             | **8.4/10** |
| **8** | Ancient trade / coins / movement                  | unexpectedly dense linked-open-data infrastructure                                   | **8.0/10** |

And several of them can actually share the **same entity graph**.

---

# 1. The biggest goldmine: the entire Tree of Life

This one is almost ridiculous.

For living animals, we have vastly more structured information than prehistoric wildlife.

**EOL TraitBank** aggregates traits, measurements and biological interactions from 50+ sources across roughly **1.7 million taxa and more than 11 million trait records**. ([Knowledge Graph Hub][1])

Then you have:

**GBIF**

```text
species
occurrences
locations
specimens
images
```

**GloBI**

```text
eats
is_eaten_by
pollinates
parasite_of
host_of
interacts_with
```

GloBI explicitly exists to aggregate open species-interaction information such as predator–prey, pollinator–plant, parasite–host and pathogen–host relationships. ([Global Biotic Interactions][2])

**Open Tree of Life**

provides an API-accessible synthesized phylogenetic tree and taxonomy. ([PubMed Central (PMC)][3])

Then image datasets are enormous. **BioTrove** alone contains about **161.9 million research-grade biodiversity images covering ~366,600 species**. ([arXiv][4])

So our generic entity could become:

```text
Animal
├── taxonomy
├── phylogeny
├── morphology
├── traits
├── habitat
├── occurrences
├── interactions
├── predators
├── prey
├── environmental tolerances
├── movement
├── imagery
└── literature
```

That is an extraordinary foundation.

### Channels generated from this one world

```text
Animal Arena
Deep Sea
Tiny Worlds
Predator Lab
Spider World
Snake World
Bird Wars
Extreme Adaptations
Evolution Lab
Ecosystem Simulator
```

Same backend.

And the content doesn't need to be fighting.

Imagine:

> **What Happens If Wolves Disappear From This Ecosystem?**

or:

> **Can This Invasive Species Take Over the Island?**

or:

> **What Happens When We Double the Predator Population?**

Those are genuinely simulations.

---

# 2. GloBI may be one of the most valuable APIs for us

This is probably the source I hadn't appreciated enough earlier.

Instead of manually inventing:

```text
lion → eats → zebra
```

GloBI gives structured biological interaction records.

It already has a JavaScript API/library for querying which organisms eat or interact with others. ([GitHub][5])

That means our world graph naturally becomes:

```text
                  leopard
                    │
                 preys_on
                    │
                    ▼
                 antelope
                    │
                  eats
                    │
                    ▼
                  plant
```

Now you can generate **real food webs**.

And then simulate perturbations.

That's a different channel:

> **I Removed One Species From the Food Web**

> **What If Jaguars Appeared Here?**

> **Can This Ecosystem Survive 100 Years?**

This is arguably a better simulator demo than animal combat because the relationships actually exist in scientific datasets.

---

# 3. Deep sea is almost purpose-built for AI content

This is probably my favorite second channel.

OBIS is a global open-access marine biodiversity system, and NOAA describes OBIS and GBIF as the world's leading open biodiversity databases, with OBIS specifically focused on marine life. ([NOAA Ocean Exploration][6])

You also have the **World Register of Deep-Sea Species**, which is specifically trying to maintain a comprehensive known deep-sea species database and collect high-quality specimen imagery. ([World Register of Marine Species][7])

Then NOAA's **World Ocean Database** gives environmental measurements; NOAA describes it as the world's largest uniformly formatted, quality-controlled public collection of ocean-profile data. ([NCEI][8])

That gives us:

```text
DeepSeaCreature
+
depth
+
temperature
+
oxygen
+
salinity
+
geography
+
known interactions
```

Then run:

> **What survives at 6,000 metres?**

> **We moved this predator 2,000 metres deeper.**

> **Which animal owns the abyss?**

> **Build the perfect deep-sea ecosystem.**

Visually, this could be insane.

And scientifically there are actual environmental variables behind it.

---

# 4. Evolution itself could be a product

Open Tree of Life is much more than a taxonomy lookup.

Its purpose is essentially to synthesize published phylogenetic information into an accessible tree of life. ([Open Tree of Life][9])

That means we can make:

```text
              ancestral node
              /           \
             /             \
         species A       species B
```

a first-class content object.

You could generate:

### Evolutionary face-offs

> How closely related are whales and hippos?

### Trait evolution

> When did venom appear?

### Alternate evolution

> What happens if environmental pressure X continues for 10,000 generations?

Clearly label the last one as simulation, not prediction.

### Evolution game

```text
environment changes
       ↓
traits have costs/benefits
       ↓
population changes
       ↓
lineage branches
```

So the same platform moves from:

```text
battle simulator
```

to:

```text
evolution simulator
```

without changing its fundamental entity/event architecture.

---

# 5. Ancient civilization is the historical jackpot

There is an enormous amount of infrastructure scattered across digital humanities projects.

### Pleiades

Pleiades provides stable identifiers and a structured gazetteer of ancient places, particularly around the Greek and Roman worlds. ([Pelagios Network][10])

### Pelagios

Pelagios has spent years linking historical texts/maps/data to geographic entities and developed tooling such as Recogito for semantic geographic annotation. ([Pleiades][11])

### OpenHistoricalMap

There is also a collaborative historical world map whose underlying data can represent features through time rather than merely the present. ([Wikipedia][12])

So the historical world could eventually contain:

```text
Place
Person
Culture
Artifact
Text
Event
Route
Institution
Settlement
```

all connected temporally.

Imagine opening:

```text
ROME
200 CE
```

and asking:

> What exists?

Then the graph determines which entities are temporally valid.

That exact mechanism later works for:

```text
Varanasi — 900 CE
Alexandria — 300 BCE
Angkor — 1150 CE
Kashmir — 1000 CE
```

This could become extremely powerful.

---

# 6. Classical mythology already has the graph we want

This was a particularly interesting discovery.

**Digital LIMC** is explicitly a **knowledge graph for classical mythology**.

As reported in 2025, it contained roughly:

* 3,000 mythological/historical figures
* 56,000 objects
* 40,500 images

linked into a structured cultural-heritage dataset. ([Journal of Open Humanities Data][13])

That's almost exactly our architecture:

```text
Zeus
├── representations
├── artifacts
├── relationships
├── associated myths
├── places
└── iconography
```

There are also efforts turning older mythology reference works into linked data; one FactGrid initiative discusses creating records for **15,000+ mythical entities** from Roscher's mythology lexicon. ([Factgrid Blog][14])

So imagine:

```text
MYTHOLOGY WORLD ENGINE
```

where a creature isn't based on generic fantasy art.

It has:

```text
source tradition
date
geographic region
textual form
iconographic form
relationships
attested attributes
```

Then you can ask:

> **How did Medusa change over 800 years?**

or:

> **Which monsters does Heracles actually encounter in surviving sources?**

or run clearly labeled counterfactual scenarios.

This is exactly the kind of infrastructure I think would eventually be incredible for Indian traditions too—but we'd have to build much more of that dataset ourselves.

---

# 7. Museum APIs are an enormous asset goldmine

This may solve a large part of historical visualization.

The Met currently makes data and public-domain imagery accessible programmatically through its API. Its Open Access initiative covers **hundreds of thousands of public-domain images**, available for unrestricted reuse. ([Met Museum API][15])

Rijksmuseum likewise exposes large-scale structured collection descriptions and image resources to developers. ([Rijksmuseum Data][16])

And this isn't isolated—the Smithsonian and multiple major museums expose API/open-data surfaces. ([Nordic APIs][17])

That means instead of asking an image model:

> “what did a 2nd-century Roman helmet look like?”

the system can first find:

```text
actual period objects
actual sculptures
actual pottery
actual textile evidence
```

and build the reconstruction from those references.

**That is how the historical-accuracy platform becomes defensible.**

---

# 8. There's already research validating the museum-graph architecture

A 2025 system called **MUSEKG** builds a typed knowledge graph over museum collections connecting objects, people, organizations and textual/visual labels, specifically to improve grounded querying over cultural heritage. ([arXiv][18])

And a July 2026 project called **VaseMuseum** goes further: multimodal AI + 2D/3D ancient artifacts + authoritative external knowledge retrieval + explicit handling of uncertain evidence. ([arXiv][19])

That is eerily close to what we've been discussing.

It strongly reinforces:

```text
SOURCE
↓
ENTITY GRAPH
↓
VISUAL ASSET
↓
GENERATIVE INTERFACE
```

instead of:

```text
prompt
↓
AI guesses history
```

---

# 9. Space is probably the cleanest simulator domain

This one has different advantages.

NASA maintains huge structured databases, including the **NASA Exoplanet Archive**, whose current mission is to maintain published information on exoplanet systems and related stellar properties. ([NASA Exoplanet Archive][20])

JPL's Small-Body Database exposes asteroid/comet properties, and NASA's open APIs expose near-Earth-object data. ([JPL Solar System Dynamics][21])

Unlike extinct-animal fights, many space questions can be modeled from extremely mature physics.

So:

> **What Happens If Jupiter Moves 10% Closer to the Sun?**

> **What If Earth Had Two Moons?**

> **We Put Every Planet Around the Same Star**

> **Can This Exoplanet Keep an Atmosphere?**

The same generic pipeline becomes:

```text
PlanetAsset
+
StarAsset
+
InitialConditions
+
Physics
↓
simulation
↓
events
↓
story
↓
cinematic visualization
```

The big advantage:

**simulation fidelity can be dramatically higher.**

The disadvantage:

less character/personality than animals.

---

# 10. Natural Earth could be another excellent simulation channel

The Smithsonian Global Volcanism Program maintains global volcano records, while USGS exposes machine-readable volcano status information and an extensive earthquake catalog. ([USGS Volcanoes][22])

So the entity graph becomes:

```text
volcano
fault
city
terrain
historic eruption
geology
```

Then:

> Why does this volcano erupt differently from that one?

> What happens if the wind changes?

> Reconstructing historic eruptions.

This one needs careful scientific modeling, but the structured-data foundation is excellent.

---

# 11. Coins sound boring until you see the data

Ancient numismatics has surprisingly advanced linked-data infrastructure.

**Nomisma.org** maintains a formal RDF ontology covering concepts such as coins, types and hoards. ([Deutsches Archäologisches Institut][23])

Linked datasets already aggregate very large numbers of Greek and Roman coins and connect them to people, places, rulers, mints and hoards. ([Academia][24])

That means coins can become sensors for history:

```text
coin
→ mint
→ ruler
→ date
→ findspot
→ hoard
→ trade route
```

Then content becomes:

> **Follow One Coin Across the Roman World**

> **How Far Did Alexander's Money Travel?**

> **What 2,000 Coin Hoards Reveal About a Collapsing Empire**

This isn't a simulator-first niche, but it uses the exact **graph → discovery → story → visualization** layer.

