# The killer automated discovery loop

Eventually I would have the engine continuously search its own worlds:

```text
ALL ENTITIES
     ↓
ALL RELATIONS
     ↓
ALL VALID SCENARIOS
     ↓
compute:
  novelty
  visuality
  uncertainty
  counterintuition
  interaction strength
  audience familiarity
     ↓
SIMULATE / INVESTIGATE
     ↓
rank results
     ↓
story proposals
```

Then instead of asking ChatGPT:

> give me 20 video ideas

you open:

```text
DISCOVERIES

#1 ★★★★★
Changing water depth reverses predator outcome.
Evidence quality: 0.82
Visual score: 0.94
Novelty: 0.88

#2 ★★★★★
Two species overlap geographically but almost never
temporally.
Historical mystery score: 0.91

#3 ★★★★☆
Trait appears independently in five distant lineages.
Evolutionary-interest score: 0.89
```

**The dataset generates the editorial calendar.**

That is where I think the actual goldmine is.

Prehistoric battles are an extremely good wedge because they force us to solve every hard primitive—entities, traits, uncertainty, arenas, interactions, simulation, persistent visuals and story compilation—while also producing immediately entertaining content. Once those primitives work, **Life Graph / Deep Sea should be the first reuse; Ancient World / Mythology should be the second; Space should be the third**. Those three expansions test biological, historical-symbolic and physical-simulation domains respectively, which would prove whether we've actually built a generic world-to-content engine rather than a dinosaur channel with fancy architecture.

